  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chefland consists of NNN provinces (numbered 111 through NNN) connected by MMM bidirectional roads (numbered 111 through MMM). For each valid iii, the iii-th road connects provinces UiU_iUi​ and ViV_iVi​. It is possible to travel from each province to any other province using these roads.\n\nFor each valid iii, the annual income of the iii-th province is AiA_iAi​ and its population is BiB_iBi​. The per-capita income of some provinces is lower than that of some other provinces. For this reason, the king of Chefland wants to choose one or more provinces to keep in his kingdom and abandon the rest, in such a way that the per-capita income of the whole resulting kingdom, i.e. the sum of annual incomes of the chosen provinces divided by the sum of their populations, would be maximum possible. However, it should also be possible to travel from each of the chosen provinces to any other chosen province without visiting any of the abandoned provinces. Among all such ways to form the new kingdom, the king would prefer the number of chosen provinces to be as large as possible.\n\nCan you help the king choose which provinces should form the new kingdom? If there are multiple solutions that maximise the per-capita income, you should choose one with the maximum number of chosen provinces. If there are still multiple solutions, you may choose any one.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤51 \\le T \\le 51≤T≤5",
      "1≤N,M≤2⋅1051 \\le N, M \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N,M≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ui,Vi≤N1 \\le U_i, V_i \\le N1≤Ui​,Vi​≤N for each valid iii",
      "Ui≠ViU_i \\neq V_iUi​=Vi​ for each valid iii",
      "there is at most one road between each pair of provinces",
      "0≤Ai≤1090 \\le A_i \\le 10^90≤Ai​≤109 for each valid iii",
      "1≤Bi≤1091 \\le B_i \\le 10^91≤Bi​≤109 for each valid iii"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\nThe third line contains NNN space-separated integers B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​.\nMMM lines follow. For each valid iii, the iii-th of these lines contains two space-separated integers UiU_iUi​ and ViV_iVi​.",
    "outputFormat": "Print two lines.\n\n\nThe first of these lines should contain a single integer KKK denoting the number of provinces the king should choose.\nThe second line should contain KKK space-separated integers denoting these provinces.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "1\n3 3\n10 1 2\n5 1 1 \n1 2\n2 3\n1 3",
        "output": "2\n1 3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nIf the king chooses provinces 111 and 333, then the per-capita income is 10+25+1=2\\frac{10 + 2}{5 + 1} = 25+110+2​=2. This is the only optimal solution."
      }
    ]
  },
